Fix $q\in(0,1]$. For each defect type $ij$, choose a positive multiplier $w_{ij}$, and define $$\label{eq:chainweights}
 \lambda(S)=
 \begin{cases}
 f_q(S),&S\in\Omega_0,\\
 w_{ij}f_q(S),&S\in\Omega_{ij},
 \end{cases}
 \qquad
 \Lambda=\sum_{S\in\Omega}\lambda(S),\qquad \pi(S)=\frac{\lambda(S)}\Lambda.$$ The conditional law on transversals is $\mu(S)=f_q(S)/c_0$ for $S\in\Omega_0$; it does not depend on the multipliers. Call the multipliers *good* if $$\label{eq:good}
 \frac14w^*_{ij}\le w_{ij}\le4w^*_{ij},
 \qquad w^*_{ij}=\frac{c_0}{c_{ij}}.$$ For good multipliers, $$\label{eq:typemasses}
 \Lambda\le5n^2c_0,\qquad
 \pi(\Omega_0)\ge\frac1{5n^2},\qquad
 \pi(\Omega_{ij})\ge\frac1{20n^2}.$$ Indeed each defect has total multiplied weight between $c_0/4$ and $4c_0$, and there are $n(n-1)$ defects.

